Step 1: convert the equation to a quartic:
\begin{equation*}
x^4 - 4kx^3 + (4k^2 - 4)x^2 + (6k - 4)kx + 16 - k^2 = 0
\end{equation*}

Step 2: find the discriminant of the quartic polynomial:
\begin{align*}
\Delta &= 16(4k^2 - 4)^2 + 4(-6k + 4)^3 + 27(16 - k^2)^2 \qquad \text{ (Ref. } \text{ Wurm 2020) } \\
&= 256(k^2 - 1)^4 + 108(9 - k^2)^2
\end{align*}

Step 3: analyze the cases:

\textbf{Case 1:} $\Delta > 0$. The equation has 4 roots by the quadratic formula.

\textbf{Case 2:} $\Delta = 0$. The equation has less than 4 roots. In this case, $k = ±1$ or $k = ±3$, and the multiplicity of a root $r$ is at least 2 (possibly 4).

\textbf{Case 3:} $\Delta < 0$. The equation has 2 equal roots and 2 complex conjugates roots. In the cases when $k = ±1$ or $k = ±3$, the roots could also coincide.

Summarize:
