The probability of rolling a 1 on a single 6-sided die is $1/6$. The probability of rolling a 2 is $1/6$ as well, and the probability of rolling either a 1 or a 2 is the sum of these probabilities, which is $2/6 + 1/6 = \boxed{1/2}$.

The number of ways to roll a 1 or a 2 with 6 dice is $6C1 * (1/6)^1 \times 6C1 * (1/6)^1 + 6C2 * (1/6)^2 = \boxed{72}$

The number of favorable ways to roll a 1 on 6 dice is $6C1 * (1/6)^6$. The number of ways to roll at least one 1 is $6C1 * (1/6)^1 + 6C2 * (1/6)^2 + \dotsb + 6C6 * (1/6)^6$

The number of ways to roll a 2 on 6 dice is $6C1 * (1/6)^6$. The number of ways to roll at least one 2 is $6C1 * (1/6)^1 + 6C2 * (1/6)^2 + \dotsb + 6C6 * (1/6)^6$

The number of ways to roll either a 1 or a 2 and at least one of them is a 2 is $6C2 * (1/6)^2 + \dotsb + 6C6 * (1/6)^6$.

The number of ways to roll at least one 1 and at least one 2 is $6C2 * (1/6)^2 + \dotsb + 6C6 * (1/6)^6$.

\boxed{(\text{number of ways to roll at least one 1 and at least one 2}) / (\text{number of ways to roll at least one 1 or 2}) - (\text{number of ways to roll a 1 or a 2 and at least one of them is a 2})/(\text{number of ways to roll at least one 1 or 2})}

The probability to roll either a 1 or a 2 and at least one of them is a 2 is:
\boxed{6C2 * (1/6)^2 + \dotsb + 6C6 * (1/6)^6}/\boxed{72}

The probability to roll at least one 1 and at least one 2 is:
\boxed{(6C2 * (1/6)^2 + \dotsb + 6C6 * (1/6)^6)/(\text{number of ways to roll at least one 1 or 2})}

Final answer:
\boxed{((6C2 * (1/6)^2 + \dotsb + 6C6 * (1/6)^6) - (6C2 * (1/6)^2 + \dotsb + 6C6 * (1/6)^6)/72)/(6C1 * (1/6) + \dotsb + 6C6 * (1/6)^6)}

