Of the smaller triangles, there are 45 "smaller ones" as stated. However, a portion of the area of each larger triangle is already occupied by these smaller ones. Since the smaller triangles have the same area as the larger ones, the total area occupied by the smaller triangles is the same as the area of one larger triangle, $A_{\text{single}}$. We subtract this from the total area of the larger triangles to find the area of the unoccupied part of the larger triangles, or the area of the remaining larger triangles:

\begin{align*}
A_{\text{remaining}} &= A_{\text{larger}} - A_{\text{small}} \\
&= (337.5 - 112.5) \\
&= \boxed{225} \text{ square inches}\\
