We can determine the hundreds digit without calculating the entire expression, but rather by applying the exponent twice to the hundreds digit of the base. Since the hundreds digit of 2 is 2, we have:

\boxed{2011^2011 \text{ ends in units digit } (2^{2011})^2 = 16\cdot (2^{2010})^2}

Now we can use the fact that $2^{10}$ is a well-known value, namely $2^{10} = 1024$, and therefore:

\boxed{16\cdot (2^{2010})^2 = 16\cdot (1024)^{2010}}

Notice that taking the cube of $1024$ gives us $1024^{3}=1,073,741,824\equiv 4$ (modulo 10), so the cube of $1024$ ends in $4$. Since taking the fourth power contains another power of $4$, this last power will contribute an additional $4$ to the units digit:

\boxed{16\cdot (1024)^{2010} \equiv 16\cdot 4 \equiv 64}

Since the units digit of $64$ is $4$, and there are finally two further powers of $16$, we reach the final result:

\boxed{2011^2011 \text{ ends in the hundreds digit } 4}

