The answer is actually simpler to find than you might think (though it does involve some cancellation).

Consider the following identity:
\begin{equation*}
    \binom{15}{7} = \frac{15!}{7!(15-7)!} = \frac{(15)(14)(13)(12)}{(7)(6)(5)(4)} = \frac{15\cdot 14\cdot 13}{4\cdot 3\cdot 2} \cdot \frac{12}{7\cdot 6} = 5\cdot 7\cdot 13 \cdot \frac{12}{7\cdot 6}
\end{equation*}
Now, notice that both the numerator and denominator have common factors of 7 and 6. We can cancel those out:

\begin{equation*}
    \boxed{\binom{15}{7} = 5\cdot 13 \cdot 2 = \boxed{10\cdot 13}}
\end{equation*}

Note that you can take the final simplification out if you like:

\begin{equation*}
    \boxed{\binom{15}{7} = \frac{15\cdot 14\cdot 13}{4\cdot 3\cdot 2} \cdot \frac{12}{7\cdot 6} = \boxed{\frac{15\cdot 14}{4\cdot 3} \cdot \frac{13\cdot 12}{6\cdot 6}}}
\end{equation*}

Either way, the four digits interpretation of the answer would be $\boxed{1013}$, with units $\boxed{}\text{ (none given in the original problem)} $.

